    "description": "Urban green spaces are both public amenities and environmental infrastructure, whose performance depends on location, accessibility, vegetation, and maintenance. Research in Tehran shows why water supply, urban expansion, and unequal provision must be considered together.",
    "tags": ["Urban parks", "Green infrastructure", "Tehran", "Water reuse", "Spatial equity"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:38:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:38:08Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Urban Green Spaces in Iran: Access, Water and Planning",
      "description": "Explore Tehran-based research on green-space provision, metropolitan land cover, and proposed wastewater reuse for park irrigation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-measures",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A green city can mean several different things",
        "content": "Urban green space is not a single, self-explanatory measurement. Research on Tehran distinguishes accessibility, quality, and quantity as separate dimensions of provision. A neighborhood’s proximity to a park therefore answers a different question from the amount of green space available or the quality of that space. These distinctions matter when interpreting claims that a district is adequately served. [s1]\n\nThe Tehran study combines geographic information systems with a weighted assessment of provision. It finds considerable inequality in green-space quantity, while reporting a more nuanced pattern for accessibility in neighborhoods with low socioeconomic status. Its findings caution against assuming that every measure will reproduce the same map of disadvantage. They also do not establish how each individual resident experiences a park. [s1]\n\nThe article’s accessible abstract supports these methodological distinctions and headline findings. Detailed neighborhood rankings and underlying observations were not inspected, so they are not reproduced here. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "metropolitan-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Parks and the wider metropolitan landscape",
        "content": "A separate study uses Landsat satellite imagery to investigate land-use and land-cover change across the Tehran Metropolitan Region. This is a wider geographic frame than an inventory of municipal public parks. Its classifications include built-up land, gardens, cultivation, water, and other land-cover categories. Those categories help describe metropolitan ecological change, but they cannot be treated as equivalent to publicly accessible recreational space. [s2]\n\nThe researchers analyze imagery from 2000, 2010, and 2020 and use cellular automata and Markov-chain modeling to explore subsequent development. The historical image classifications and modeled future conditions are different kinds of evidence. A projection is not an observation of land already converted, protected, or planted. Nor do these separated image dates provide a continuous annual record. [s2]\n\nFor an international reader, the central distinction is between vegetation visible across a metropolitan region and a usable public amenity near home. The first study evaluates dimensions of urban provision; the second examines regional land cover. Their findings are complementary, but their geographic boundaries, categories, and methods should remain separate. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "irrigation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keeping green space alive under water constraints",
        "content": "Research published in September 2026 examines a proposed decentralized irrigation supply for Tehran’s parks. Instead of relying on long pipelines from centralized treatment plants, the model places local treatment units near parks and connects them to suitable points on existing trunk sewers. Gravity-fed conveyance is a central design condition. [s3]\n\nThis is an optimization study, not evidence that the proposed network has been constructed. Its economic conclusions depend particularly on treatment operating costs. The authors recommend a monitored pilot, checks of sewer elevation records, and continuing verification of treated-water quality against irrigation standards. Those qualifications are essential: an attractive modeled layout does not establish actual treatment performance or safe operating conditions. [s3]\n\nRead together, these studies suggest that urban greening should be assessed through provision, metropolitan land change, and the resources required for maintenance. Adding mapped vegetation alone cannot answer whether public space is accessible or whether its irrigation system is sustainable. That conclusion is an editorial synthesis of the distinct questions investigated by the sources, rather than a national performance score. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "proposed-reuse",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The proposed local wastewater-to-irrigation arrangement",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Existing trunk sewer\"] -->|\"Proposed diversion\"| B[\"Selected branch connection\"]\n  B -->|\"Gravity conveyance\"| C[\"Local treatment unit near park\"]\n  C -->|\"Treatment and quality verification\"| D[\"Reclaimed irrigation water\"]\n  D -->|\"Proposed irrigation supply\"| E[\"Urban park\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe the proposed physical water pathway in a Tehran optimization study. They do not represent a verified operating network or institutional reporting relationships.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
